  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":29,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A school canteen uses 15 kg of rice for 120 meals. A second canteen uses 26 kg for 200 meals.\n\n(a) Calculate the rice used per meal at the first canteen, in grams.\n(b) Calculate the rice used per meal at the second canteen, in grams.\n(c) State which canteen uses less rice per meal and by how many grams, and explain why comparing the totals 15 kg and 26 kg would be misleading.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A school canteen uses 15 kg of rice for 120 meals. A second canteen uses 26 kg for 200 meals."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the rice used per meal at the first canteen, in grams.\n(b) Calculate the rice used per meal at the second canteen, in grams.\n(c) State which canteen uses less rice per meal and by how many grams, and explain why comparing the totals 15 kg and 26 kg would be misleading."}]}]},"e":"15 000 g ÷ 120 = 125 g and 26 000 g ÷ 200 = 130 g, so the first canteen uses 5 g less per meal. A total is not a rate: the second canteen serves 80 more meals, so its bigger total does not mean it wastes more per meal.","m":4,"df":"hard","tp":"ratio","st":"ratio","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":29,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"To compare two groups of different size, compare a per-item rate. Convert units before dividing.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the grams of rice per meal at the first canteen.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Convert 15 kg to grams first.","Divide the grams by 120 meals."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":125,"tolerance":0,"unit":"g"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the grams of rice per meal at the second canteen.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Convert 26 kg to grams.","Divide by 200 meals."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":130,"tolerance":0,"unit":"g"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"State which canteen uses less per meal, by how many grams, and why totals mislead.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Compare the two per-meal values, not the two totals.","The canteens serve different numbers of meals."],"strand":"D(ii)","accepted":["the first canteen, by 5 g per meal, because the canteens serve different numbers of meals so the totals are not comparable"],"keywords":[["first"],["canteen"],["meal"],["canteens"],["serve"],["different"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 125 g. (b) 1 mark for 130 g. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''first canteen, 5 g less'', 1 for explaining that the meal counts differ.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 125 g. (b) 1 mark for 130 g. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''first canteen, 5 g less'', 1 for explaining that the meal counts differ.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Show the unit conversion in (a) and (b). In (c) give the difference AND the reason."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 125 g. (b) 1 mark for 130 g. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''first canteen, 5 g less'', 1 for explaining that the meal counts differ."},{"n":30,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A photograph is 15 cm wide and 10 cm high. It is enlarged so that the width becomes 39 cm.\n\n(a) Calculate the scale factor of the enlargement.\n(b) Calculate the new height.\n(c) Explain what assumption your answer depends on, and what would happen to the picture if that assumption were false.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A photograph is 15 cm wide and 10 cm high. It is enlarged so that the width becomes 39 cm."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the scale factor of the enlargement.\n(b) Calculate the new height.\n(c) Explain what assumption your answer depends on, and what would happen to the picture if that assumption were false."}]}]},"e":"39 ÷ 15 = 2.6, so the height becomes 10 × 2.6 = 26 cm. This requires similar (proportional) enlargement. Without that assumption a designer could set any height, and the photograph would be distorted.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"proportion","st":"proportion","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":30,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"An enlargement uses one scale factor for every dimension. State the similarity assumption when a real object is modelled.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the scale factor.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Divide the new width by the original width.","39 ÷ 15."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":2.6,"tolerance":0.001},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the new height, in centimetres.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Multiply the original height by the scale factor.","Use the same factor as the width."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":26,"tolerance":0,"unit":"cm"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"State the assumption and what would go wrong without it.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["A photograph that is enlarged without distortion keeps its proportions.","If width and height used different factors, the shape would change."],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["the shape is preserved, so both dimensions use the same scale factor; otherwise the picture would be stretched"],"keywords":[["shape"],["preserved"],["dimensions"],["scale"],["factor"],["otherwise"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 2.6. (b) 2 marks: 1 for multiplying by the factor, 1 for 26 cm. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''proportions preserved'', 1 for the consequence of distortion.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 2.6. (b) 2 marks: 1 for multiplying by the factor, 1 for 26 cm. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''proportions preserved'', 1 for the consequence of distortion.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","geometry"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"One scale factor is used for both dimensions in (b) — say why that is legitimate in (c)."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 2.6. (b) 2 marks: 1 for multiplying by the factor, 1 for 26 cm. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''proportions preserved'', 1 for the consequence of distortion."},{"n":31,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A game at a school fair costs $3 per play. It uses a bag of 12 balls: 1 gold ball wins $15, 3 silver balls win $3 each and the remaining 8 win nothing.\n\n(a) Write P(gold) as a fraction and as a decimal.\n(b) Calculate the expected winnings per play.\n(c) Calculate the expected gain or loss per play and decide whether the organiser should expect to make a profit.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A game at a school fair costs "},{"type":"text","text":"$3 per play. It uses a bag of 12 balls: 1 gold ball wins $"},{"type":"text","text":"15, 3 silver balls win "},{"type":"text","text":"$3 each and the remaining 8 win nothing."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Write P(gold) as a fraction and as a decimal.\n(b) Calculate the expected winnings per play.\n(c) Calculate the expected gain or loss per play and decide whether the organiser should expect to make a profit."}]}]},"e":"E(winnings) = (1/12)(15) + (3/12)(3) = 1.25 + 0.75 = $2.00 against a $3 charge, so the player''s expected loss — and the organiser''s expected profit — is $1.00 per play.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"probability","st":"probability","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":31,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Expected value = Σ(prize × probability). A loss for the player is a profit for the organiser: state which one you are computing.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Write P(gold) as a decimal, to 3 decimal places.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Probability = favourable outcomes ÷ total outcomes.","One gold ball out of 12 balls, so evaluate 1 ÷ 12."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":0.083,"tolerance":0.001},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the expected winnings per play, in dollars.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Multiply each prize by its probability and add the products.","(1/12) × 15 plus (3/12) × 3."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":2.0,"tolerance":0.02,"unit":"$"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the expected profit per play for the organiser, and justify it.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Compare the expected winnings with the $3 price.","The organiser keeps the price and pays out the winnings."],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["the player expects to lose $1.00 per play, so the organiser expects to make about $1.00 profit per play"],"keywords":[["player"],["expects"],["lose"],["play"],["organiser"],["profit"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 0.083. (b) 2 marks: 1 for both products, 1 for $2.00. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the comparison with $3, 1 for identifying it as the organiser''s profit.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 0.083. (b) 2 marks: 1 for both products, 1 for $2.00. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the comparison with $3, 1 for identifying it as the organiser''s profit.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Keep the fractions exact in (b) before converting, and state in (c) whose expected value you are describing."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 0.083. (b) 2 marks: 1 for both products, 1 for $2.00. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the comparison with $3, 1 for identifying it as the organiser''s profit."},{"n":32,"t":"assess_parts","p":"In a class of 30 students, 17 study French (F), 15 study Spanish (S) and 6 study neither language.\n\n(a) Calculate how many students study at least one language.\n(b) Calculate how many study both languages.\n(c) A student claims that 11 students study French but not Spanish. Evaluate this claim.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In a class of 30 students, 17 study French (F), 15 study Spanish (S) and 6 study neither language."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate how many students study at least one language.\n(b) Calculate how many study both languages.\n(c) A student claims that 11 students study French but not Spanish. Evaluate this claim."}]}]},"e":"At least one = 24. The overlap is 17 + 15 − 24 = 8, so French only = 9 and Spanish only = 7 (9 + 8 + 7 = 24). The suggested 11 confuses the ''neither'' group with the overlap.","m":6,"df":"hard","tp":"sets and Venn diagrams","st":"sets and Venn diagrams","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":32,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Use n(F ∪ S) = n(F) + n(S) − n(F ∩ S), and remember the ''neither'' group is outside both circles.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate how many students study at least one language.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Everyone in the class either studies a language or studies neither.","So subtract the 6 who study neither from the 30 in the class."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":24,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate how many students study both languages.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["n(F ∪ S) = n(F) + n(S) − n(F ∩ S).","17 + 15 − 24."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":8,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Evaluate the claim that 11 students study French but not Spanish.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["French only = n(F) − n(F ∩ S).","Compare 17 − 8 with 17 − 6."],"strand":"D(iii)","accepted":["the claim is wrong: french only is 17 − 8 = 9, not 11; subtracting 6 subtracts the wrong group"],"keywords":[["claim"],["wrong"],["french"],["subtracting"],["subtracts"],["group"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 24. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the union formula, 1 for 8. (c) 3 marks: 1 for French only = 9, 1 for identifying the misused group, 1 for a full evaluation.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 24. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the union formula, 1 for 8. (c) 3 marks: 1 for French only = 9, 1 for identifying the misused group, 1 for a full evaluation.","response_tools":["venn","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"venn","response_guidance":"Fill the overlap first in the workspace, then the two ''only'' regions, then place the 6 outside."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 24. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the union formula, 1 for 8. (c) 3 marks: 1 for French only = 9, 1 for identifying the misused group, 1 for a full evaluation."},{"n":33,"t":"assess_parts","p":"Three brands of washing powder are sold in different pack sizes.\n\nPack A: 1.2 kg for $6.60\nPack B: 2 kg for $10.50\nPack C: 3 kg for $16.20\n\n(a) Calculate the cost per kilogram of Pack A.\n(b) Calculate the cost per kilogram of Pack C.\n(c) State which pack is the best buy, and explain why a household that does one wash a week might not choose it.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Three brands of washing powder are sold in different pack sizes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Pack A: 1.2 kg for "},{"type":"text","text":"$6.60\nPack B: 2 kg for $"},{"type":"text","text":"10.50\nPack C: 3 kg for "},{"type":"text","text":"$16.20"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the cost per kilogram of Pack A.\n(b) Calculate the cost per kilogram of Pack C.\n(c) State which pack is the best buy, and explain why a household that does one wash a week might not choose it."}]}]},"e":"Unit prices: A $5.50, B $5.25, C $5.40. Pack B is the best buy even though it is neither the largest nor the smallest; the largest pack (C) is dearer per kilogram. Usage matters because powder can spoil before a small household finishes 3 kg.","m":6,"df":"hard","tp":"rate","st":"rate","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":33,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Best buy means the lowest unit price — compute every option before choosing, and check that the quantity will be used.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the cost per kilogram of Pack A.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Divide the price by the mass in kilograms.","6.60 ÷ 1.2."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":5.5,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"$/kg"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the cost per kilogram of Pack C.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Divide the price of the pack by its mass in kilograms.","Pack C costs $16.20 for 3 kg, so evaluate 16.20 ÷ 3."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":5.4,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"$/kg"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"State the best buy and why a small household might choose differently.","kind":"text","points":4,"hints":["Compare all three unit prices before deciding.","Does ''biggest'' always mean ''best value''?"],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["pack b at $5.25 per kg is the best unit price, but a household using 3 kg slowly may waste or spoil powder, so the cheapest per kilogram is not automatically the best purchase"],"keywords":[["pack"],["best"],["unit"],["price"],["household"],["using"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 4 marks: 1 for Pack B, 1 for the $5.25 comparison, 1 for noting that C is dearer per kg despite being largest, 1 for the waste argument.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 4 marks: 1 for Pack B, 1 for the $5.25 comparison, 1 for noting that C is dearer per kg despite being largest, 1 for the waste argument.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Give all three unit prices in your working, even though only two are boxed — the comparison in (c) needs them."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 4 marks: 1 for Pack B, 1 for the $5.25 comparison, 1 for noting that C is dearer per kg despite being largest, 1 for the waste argument."},{"n":34,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A recipe for 12 flapjacks uses oats, butter and honey in the ratio 6 : 3 : 1 by mass, and produces 12 flapjacks each of mass 80 g.\n\n(a) Calculate the total mass of one batch.\n(b) Calculate the mass of oats in one batch.\n(c) A cook has 900 g of oats and wants to make as many flapjacks as possible, with the other ingredients unlimited. Calculate the number of complete batches possible.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A recipe for 12 flapjacks uses oats, butter and honey in the ratio 6 : 3 : 1 by mass, and produces 12 flapjacks each of mass 80 g."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the total mass of one batch.\n(b) Calculate the mass of oats in one batch.\n(c) A cook has 900 g of oats and wants to make as many flapjacks as possible, with the other ingredients unlimited. Calculate the number of complete batches possible."}]}]},"e":"A batch is 960 g with 576 g of oats (6/10 of the mass). 900 ÷ 576 ≈ 1.56, so 1 complete batch is possible; 2 batches need 1152 g. Rounding up would promise flapjacks the oats cannot supply.","m":7,"df":"hard","tp":"ratio","st":"ratio","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":34,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"In a ratio, divide by the TOTAL number of parts. When scaling a recipe, round down to whole batches.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the total mass of one batch, in grams.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Multiply the mass of one flapjack by 12.","12 × 80."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":960,"tolerance":0,"unit":"g"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the mass of oats in one batch, in grams.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The ratio parts total 6 + 3 + 1 = 10.","Divide 960 by 10, then multiply by 6."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":576,"tolerance":0,"unit":"g"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the number of complete batches possible with 900 g of oats.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Divide the available oats by the oats per batch.","900 ÷ 576 = 1.56, so how many complete batches?"],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":1,"tolerance":0,"unit":"batches"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Explain why the cook cannot make 1.56 batches.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Can you bake a fraction of a batch and still have complete trays?","What would two full batches require?"],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["a batch is a whole number of flapjacks: only 1 complete batch fits, because 2 batches would need 1152 g of oats and the cook has 900 g"],"keywords":[["batch"],["whole"],["number"],["flapjacks"],["complete"],["fits"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the 10 parts, 1 for 576 g. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for 1. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the whole-batch argument, 1 for citing 1152 g.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the 10 parts, 1 for 576 g. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for 1. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the whole-batch argument, 1 for citing 1152 g.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Do not round 1.56 up — explain in (d) why the answer must be a whole number of batches."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the 10 parts, 1 for 576 g. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for 1. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the whole-batch argument, 1 for citing 1152 g."},{"n":35,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A seed packet claims that 85% of its seeds germinate. A gardener plants 20 seeds and 14 germinate.\n\n(a) Calculate the relative frequency of germination in the gardener''s trial.\n(b) The gardener concludes the packet''s claim is false. Explain why this conclusion is unsafe for a sample of 20.\n(c) Calculate how many of the 20 seeds you would expect to germinate if the claim were true, and use it to judge the gardener''s result.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A seed packet claims that 85% of its seeds germinate. A gardener plants 20 seeds and 14 germinate."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the relative frequency of germination in the gardener''s trial.\n(b) The gardener concludes the packet''s claim is false. Explain why this conclusion is unsafe for a sample of 20.\n(c) Calculate how many of the 20 seeds you would expect to germinate if the claim were true, and use it to judge the gardener''s result."}]}]},"e":"The observed relative frequency is 0.70 against a claim of 0.85, an expected 17 of 20. A shortfall of 3 is within ordinary sampling variation for such a small sample, so the claim is not disproved; a trial of several hundred seeds would be needed.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"probability","st":"probability","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":35,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Judge a probability claim against the expected count AND the size of the sample; small samples vary widely.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the relative frequency of germination, as a decimal.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Divide the germinated seeds by the number planted.","14 ÷ 20."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":0.7,"tolerance":0.001},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the number of seeds expected to germinate if the claim is true.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Multiply the claimed probability by the 20 seeds.","0.85 × 20."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":17,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Explain why a result of 14 does not disprove the claim.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["Compare the observed 14 with the expected 17.","Would a different handful of 20 seeds give a different count?"],"strand":"D(iii)","accepted":["14 is only 3 below the expected 17, and with a sample of only 20 that shortfall is ordinary random variation, so a larger trial is needed before rejecting the claim","the sample is too small: random variation in 20 seeds easily produces a difference of 3"],"keywords":[[["standard deviation","spread","variation","variability","consisten"],["smaller","larger","closer","more","less","compare","mean"]],[["sample","small","few","20 spins","20 seeds","200 spins","variation","chance"],["too small","random","not enough","larger","more trials","expected","difference"]]],"minKeywordGroups":1}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark for 17. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the comparison, 1 for sampling variation, 1 for the recommendation of a larger sample.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark for 17. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the comparison, 1 for sampling variation, 1 for the recommendation of a larger sample.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Quote the expected count in (c) — the judgement is a comparison with it, not an opinion."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark for 17. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the comparison, 1 for sampling variation, 1 for the recommendation of a larger sample."},{"n":36,"t":"assess_parts","p":"In a survey of 50 students about three sports, 24 play football (F), 20 play hockey (H) and 9 play both.\n\n(a) Calculate how many play football only.\n(b) Calculate how many play exactly one of the two sports.\n(c) State how many play neither, and describe the region (F ∪ H)′ in words.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In a survey of 50 students about three sports, 24 play football (F), 20 play hockey (H) and 9 play both."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate how many play football only.\n(b) Calculate how many play exactly one of the two sports.\n(c) State how many play neither, and describe the region (F ∪ H)′ in words."}]}]},"e":"Overlap 9, football only 15, hockey only 11, so exactly one sport = 26 and the union is 35. Neither = 50 − 35 = 15. The complement of the union is exactly that ''neither'' region.","m":7,"df":"hard","tp":"sets and Venn diagrams","st":"sets and Venn diagrams","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":36,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Fill the overlap first; ''exactly one'' means the two ''only'' regions and excludes the overlap.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate how many students play football only.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The 24 footballers include everyone who plays both sports.","Subtract the 9 who play both from the 24."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":15,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate how many students play exactly one of the two sports.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Add the football-only and hockey-only regions.","(24 − 9) + (20 − 9)."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":26,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate how many students play neither sport.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["First add all three regions inside the circles.","Subtract that union from 50."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":15,"tolerance":0},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Describe (F ∪ H)′ in words.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["The dash means ''the complement'': everything outside.","Outside both circles means neither sport."],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["students who play neither football nor hockey"],"keywords":[["students"],["play"],["neither"],["football"],["hockey"]],"minKeywordGroups":3}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for both only-regions, 1 for 26. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the union 35, 1 for 15. (d) 2 marks: 1 for ''neither'', 1 for describing it as outside both sets.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for both only-regions, 1 for 26. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the union 35, 1 for 15. (d) 2 marks: 1 for ''neither'', 1 for describing it as outside both sets.","response_tools":["venn","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"venn","response_guidance":"Place the 9 in the overlap first, then the two ''only'' regions, then the 15 outside the circles."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for both only-regions, 1 for 26. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the union 35, 1 for 15. (d) 2 marks: 1 for ''neither'', 1 for describing it as outside both sets."},{"n":37,"t":"assess_parts","p":"Six identical machines take 10 days to complete an order.\n\n(a) Calculate the total number of machine-days needed for the order.\n(b) Calculate how many days four machines would take at the same rate.\n(c) After 4 days, two more machines are added to the original six. Calculate how many further days are needed.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Six identical machines take 10 days to complete an order."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the total number of machine-days needed for the order.\n(b) Calculate how many days four machines would take at the same rate.\n(c) After 4 days, two more machines are added to the original six. Calculate how many further days are needed."}]}]},"e":"The order is 60 machine-days. Four machines take 15 days. In 4 days the six machines finish 24 machine-days, leaving 36; eight machines then need 36 ÷ 8 = 4.5 further days.","m":8,"df":"hard","tp":"proportion","st":"proportion","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":37,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"In inverse-proportion work problems, convert to machine-days (or person-days) first, then divide by the new rate.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the total number of machine-days needed.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["One machine working for one day completes one machine-day of work.","Six machines working for ten days: multiply the two numbers."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":60,"tolerance":0,"unit":"machine-days"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate how many days four machines would take.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The order still needs the same total machine-days of work.","With four machines, divide the machine-days by four."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":15,"tolerance":0,"unit":"days"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the work completed in the first 4 days, in machine-days.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Count the machine-days completed before the extra machines arrive.","Six machines each work for four days."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":24,"tolerance":0,"unit":"machine-days"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Calculate how many further days the eight machines need.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Subtract 24 from 60 to find the work left.","With two extra machines there are 8 working at once: divide by 8."],"strand":"D(iv)","answer":4.5,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"days"}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for 15. (c) 2 marks: 1 for 6 × 4, 1 for 24. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the remaining 36, 1 for using 8 machines, 1 for 4.5.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for 15. (c) 2 marks: 1 for 6 × 4, 1 for 24. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the remaining 36, 1 for using 8 machines, 1 for 4.5.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Work in machine-days throughout; never add the days of different machines as if they were separate."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for 15. (c) 2 marks: 1 for 6 × 4, 1 for 24. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the remaining 36, 1 for using 8 machines, 1 for 4.5."},{"n":38,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A charity lottery sells 800 tickets. One ticket wins $200 and five tickets win $20 each. A ticket costs $2.\n\n(a) Write the probability of winning the $200 prize as a decimal.\n(b) Calculate the expected winnings from one ticket.\n(c) A supporter argues that buying 10 tickets makes the game fair. Evaluate this argument using expected values.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A charity lottery sells 800 tickets. One ticket wins "},{"type":"text","text":"$200 and five tickets win $"},{"type":"text","text":"20 each. A ticket costs "},{"type":"text","text":"$2."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Write the probability of winning the "},{"type":"text","text":"$200 prize as a decimal.\n(b) Calculate the expected winnings from one ticket.\n(c) A supporter argues that buying 10 tickets makes the game fair. Evaluate this argument using expected values."}]}]},"e":"Expected winnings per ticket = 0.00125(200) + 0.00625(20) = $0.375 against a $2 cost, an expected loss of $1.625 per ticket. Ten tickets cost $20 and return $3.75 in expectation, so the game remains firmly unfair.","m":6,"df":"hard","tp":"probability","st":"probability","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":38,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Expected value is linear: multiplying the number of tickets multiplies both the cost and the expectation, leaving the expected loss per ticket unchanged.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Write the probability of winning the $200 prize as a decimal.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["One ticket out of 800.","1 ÷ 800."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":0.00125,"tolerance":1e-05},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the expected winnings from one ticket, in dollars.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Multiply each prize by its probability and add.","0.00125 × 200 + 0.00625 × 20."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":0.375,"tolerance":0.005,"unit":"$"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Evaluate the argument that 10 tickets make the game fair.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["What do 10 tickets cost?","How does multiplying the tickets affect the expected winnings?"],"strand":"D(iii)","accepted":["the argument fails: 10 tickets cost $20 and return only $3.75 in expectation, a loss of $16.25, and buying more tickets scales the cost and the expected winnings equally so the game stays unfair"],"keywords":[["argument"],["fails"],["tickets"],["cost"],["return"],["expectation"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the products, 1 for $0.375. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the cost of 10 tickets, 1 for the expected return, 1 for the conclusion that scaling does not make it fair.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the products, 1 for $0.375. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the cost of 10 tickets, 1 for the expected return, 1 for the conclusion that scaling does not make it fair.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"In (c) compute the cost AND the expected return for 10 tickets; the comparison is the argument."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the products, 1 for $0.375. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the cost of 10 tickets, 1 for the expected return, 1 for the conclusion that scaling does not make it fair."},{"n":39,"t":"assess_parts","p":"Two students simplify the ratio 45 : 60 : 75.\n\nAisha writes 3 : 4 : 5. Ben writes 9 : 12 : 15.\n\n(a) State the highest common factor of 45, 60 and 75.\n(b) Explain which of the two answers is fully simplified and why the other is not wrong but incomplete.\n(c) The ratio represents the prize money of three winners sharing $540. Calculate each winner''s share using the simplified ratio.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two students simplify the ratio 45 : 60 : 75."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Aisha writes 3 : 4 : 5. Ben writes 9 : 12 : 15."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the highest common factor of 45, 60 and 75.\n(b) Explain which of the two answers is fully simplified and why the other is not wrong but incomplete.\n(c) The ratio represents the prize money of three winners sharing "},{"type":"text","text":"$540. Calculate each winner''s share using the simplified ratio."}]}]},"e":"Dividing 45 : 60 : 75 by 15 gives 3 : 4 : 5, so Aisha is fully simplified while Ben stopped at 9 : 12 : 15. The parts total 12, one part is $45, so the shares are $135, $180 and $225.","m":6,"df":"hard","tp":"ratio","st":"ratio","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":39,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Divide by the HCF, not just any common factor, and check that no common factor remains.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the highest common factor of 45, 60 and 75.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["List the factors of 45 and look for the largest one shared by all three.","15 divides all three; is any larger number a factor of all three?"],"strand":"C(i)","answer":15,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Explain which answer is fully simplified, and why the other is not wrong.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Which ratio cannot be divided by a common factor any more?","Equivalent ratios can be correct without being simplest form."],"strand":"C(ii)","accepted":["aisha''s 3 : 4 : 5 is fully simplified; ben''s 9 : 12 : 15 is an equivalent ratio but still divisible by 3, so it is not in simplest form"],"keywords":[["aisha''s"],["fully"],["simplified"],["ben''s"],["equivalent"],["ratio"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the value of one part, in dollars.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The simplified parts total 3 + 4 + 5 = 12.","Divide $540 by 12."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":45,"tolerance":0,"unit":"$"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"State the largest share, in dollars.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The largest share is 5 parts.","Multiply your part value by 5."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":225,"tolerance":0,"unit":"$"}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for naming Aisha''s ratio, 1 for explaining equivalence. (c) 1 mark for $45. (d) 2 marks: 1 for 5 parts, 1 for $225.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for naming Aisha''s ratio, 1 for explaining equivalence. (c) 1 mark for $45. (d) 2 marks: 1 for 5 parts, 1 for $225.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Show the part value in (c); both shares follow from it, and it earns its own mark."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for naming Aisha''s ratio, 1 for explaining equivalence. (c) 1 mark for $45. (d) 2 marks: 1 for 5 parts, 1 for $225."},{"n":40,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A bag contains 4 red and 6 green counters. A counter is drawn and NOT replaced, then a second counter is drawn.\n\n(a) State the probability that the first counter is red.\n(b) Given the first counter was red, state the probability that the second is also red.\n(c) Calculate the probability that both counters are red.\n(d) Explain why your answer in (c) is not simply (4/10) × (4/10).","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A bag contains 4 red and 6 green counters. A counter is drawn and NOT replaced, then a second counter is drawn."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the probability that the first counter is red.\n(b) Given the first counter was red, state the probability that the second is also red.\n(c) Calculate the probability that both counters are red.\n(d) Explain why your answer in (c) is not simply (4/10) × (4/10)."}]}]},"e":"P(R) = 4/10; given a red is removed, P(R) = 3/9. Both red = 12/90 = 2/15. Squaring 4/10 assumes replacement, which would keep all 10 counters in the bag.","m":7,"df":"hard","tp":"probability","st":"probability","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":40,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Without replacement, the denominator falls by one and the numerator falls for the colour drawn: multiply along the branches.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the probability that the first counter is red, as a decimal.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Probability = red counters ÷ total counters.","There are 4 red counters and 10 in the bag altogether."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":0.4,"tolerance":0.001},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"State the probability that the second is red given the first was red, as a decimal.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["One red has been removed, so how many red and how many total remain?","3 red out of 9."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":0.333,"tolerance":0.002},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the probability that both counters are red, as a fraction in simplest form.","kind":"expression","points":2,"hints":["Multiply the two probabilities.","(4/10) × (3/9) simplifies to what?"],"strand":"C(iii)","accepted":["2/15"]},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Explain why the answer is not (4/10) × (4/10).","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Is the first counter put back?","What happens to the total number of counters?"],"strand":"C(iv)","accepted":["the first counter is not replaced, so the second draw is from 9 counters and the probability changes; (4/10)² would be correct only with replacement"],"keywords":[["first"],["counter"],["replaced"],["second"],["draw"],["counters"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 3/9, 1 for 0.333. (c) 2 marks: 1 for multiplying, 1 for 2/15. (d) 2 marks: 1 for ''no replacement'', 1 for the independence consequence.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 3/9, 1 for 0.333. (c) 2 marks: 1 for multiplying, 1 for 2/15. (d) 2 marks: 1 for ''no replacement'', 1 for the independence consequence.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"A tree diagram in the workspace makes the changing denominators visible — build it before (c)."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 3/9, 1 for 0.333. (c) 2 marks: 1 for multiplying, 1 for 2/15. (d) 2 marks: 1 for ''no replacement'', 1 for the independence consequence."}]'::jsonb) as x
